 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of sample steps $k_{max}$. Each step involves a distance field query and vector arithmetic.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Constant $O(1)$ auxiliary memory, as only a few scalar and vector variables are maintained.
-*   **Strengths:** Better approximates sky visibility effects compared to standard ray marching AO, making sphere-traced images resemble reference images more closely for curved surfaces like spheres [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Not robust across different geometries; requires manual tuning of parameters ($\alpha$, $s$, $k_{max}$) for different scene types. Can produce artifacts like incorrect highlighting on box-like structures if applied uniformly [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When rendering curved surfaces (e.g., spheres) where sky visibility is important and the geometry is isolated or can be handled with specific parameters.
-*   **When not to use:** For ground planes or box-like structures where standard AO performs better, or when a robust, parameter-free solution is required [S1, S2, unofficial].
